{"version":1,"kind":"summary","errors":11,"warnings":0,"explanations":{"E0132":"# E0132: range written another language's way\n\nA range is written `a..b`, which leaves out `b`, or `a..=b`, which includes it. Sisu has no `a..<b` and no `a...b`.\n\n## Why\n\n`..` already leaves out its end in a `for` loop, so an inclusive `..`, as some languages spell it, would change the loops that exist; Rust's `..=` adds the inclusive form without that. Both work in a `for` loop and in a pattern. `0..<n` means what `0..n` means, so the fix deletes the `<`. `1...5` could mean `1..=5` or `1..5`, so the report names both and offers no fix.\n\nA slice written with a colon, as Python and Go write it, is the same mistake. `xs[1:3]` means `xs[1..3]`, `xs[1:]` means `xs[1..]`, `xs[:3]` means `xs[..3]`, and `xs[:]` means `xs`. The fix replaces the `:` with `..`, and removes `[:]`. A step, as in `xs[::2]`, has no equal in Sisu.\n\nSisu has no negative index. In Python, `xs[:-1]` counts from the end. The report offers no fix, because the parser cannot see the type of `xs`, and a bound can change `xs`. When the value before `[` is a name, or starts at a name or `self` (as `c.xs` or `xss[0]`), the report names its `len()`, as `xs.len()`, to count from. On a string, `len()` and a range count bytes.\n\n## Broken\n\n```sisu\nfn main() {\n    for i in 0..<3 {\n        print(i)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Fixed\n\n```sisu\nfn main() {\n    for i in 0..3 {\n        print(i)\n    }\n}\n```\n"}}
